LVM is a system of partitioning disks to allow for easy management later (there's a whole bunch of benefits but I won't go into them here).  It's made up of PV's (physical volumes, i.e. disks)which contain VG's (volume groups) which in turn contain LV's (Logical volumes).

&amp;#x200B;

In this case, /dev/sda3 is a PV which is filled by the "pve" VGS.  That in turn contains the logical volumes "data", "root" and "swap".  You can think of the later two as partitions, formatted and used as you would expect.

&amp;#x200B;

The "data" LV is a thin volume where volumes will be created when you create VM's or containers (your given the option to use local-lvm during creation).  Right now it's showing 0% used (the Data% column), so that's where your free space is (apart from the free space in your / partition).

&amp;#x200B;

You'll end up with something like this (from one of my development nodes)

&amp;#x200B;

      LV            VG  Attr       LSize   Pool Origin Data%  Meta%  Move Log Cpy%Sync Convert
      data          pve twi-aotz-- 500.00g             8.84   4.43                            
      root          pve -wi-ao----  75.00g                                                    
      swap          pve -wi-ao----  32.00g                                                    
      vm-100-disk-0 pve Vwi-aotz-- 100.00g data        18.19                                  
      vm-101-disk-0 pve Vwi-a-tz--  32.00g data        15.06                                  
      vm-102-disk-0 pve Vwi-a-tz-- 100.00g data        3.67                                   
      vm-103-disk-0 pve Vwi-aotz--  64.00g data        8.66                                   
      vm-104-disk-0 pve Vwi-a-tz-- 128.00g data        9.35   

&amp;#x200B;

You can see the virtual machine volumes (begining VM-) are listed as in the data pool and the usage of my data pool is at 8.84% (being a thin volume it only allocates disk space actually being used and not space that is free).

&amp;#x200B;

Hopefully that clears things up for you.

Okay, so it looks like you just need to tell proxmox to use the logical volume.

&amp;#x200B;

I can't understand German (I think that's what those screenshots are in), but hopefully you'll be able to follow along.

&amp;#x200B;

In the Storage section under Datacenter, select Add then LVM-Thin from the drop down.  Enter whatever you want for ID, thren select "pve" for volume group and "data" for thin pool.  Set whatever you want for the Content and Nodes and click Add.

&amp;#x200B;

Hopefully you should see an entry for the value you set in ID in the Storage drop down when creating a VM

[https://grafana.com/dashboards/10048](https://grafana.com/dashboards/10048) 

&amp;#x200B;

This is pretty much everything you can do (at least that I figured out) from just the external stats that proxmox produces.

The shield will play pretty much anything you throw at it, so you probably won't need something powerfull enough to transcode.

&amp;#x200B;

I use a TS-251+ (Which also runs couchpotato, sickchill and nzbget), TS-431 and a TS-453A to run plex servers and, appart from some really high bitrate 4k movies stuttering, I don't have any problems.  Any of the devices you specify should be able to handle what you want to do with no problem.

You'd be best to read up on NAT [here.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Network_address_translation)  But basically it means routing everything via one IP address.  It has the downside that only one of your VM's can use a specific external port, so if you want two VM's to host an HTTP server, only one would be available on the standard port 80 and the other would have to use a different port.  Plus you have to manage forwarding on the host that actually has the external IP address.  It's useful if you really don't have access to additional IP addresses, but I'd avoid it if at all possible.

&amp;#x200B;

For the IPv6 route (and please be aware that IPv6 is not exaclty common, some of your friends may not have IPv6), you could use the config above, give each VM an unque address within the /64 you have, a "64" netmask and set the gateway to the 2a01:xxx:xxx:1245::2 address.

